\section*{The entropy photon-number inequality for arbitrary finite-mode inputs}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Independent finite-energy bosonic inputs are claimed to satisfy the entropy photon-number inequality in any finite number of modes, allowing internal multimode entanglement.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For finite \(n\ge1\), take independent input states \(\rho_A\otimes\rho_B\) on two collections of \(n\) bosonic modes, each with finite total mean photon number. Internal correlations among the modes of either input are allowed. Let \(\rho_C\) be the output on the modes \(c_j=\sqrt\eta\,a_j+\sqrt{1-\eta}\,b_j\), \(0\le\eta\le1\). Define \(g(t)=(t+1)\log(t+1)-t\log t\) and von Neumann entropy \(S\) in nats. The asserted inequality is
\[
 g^{-1}(S(\rho_C)/n)\ge
 \eta g^{-1}(S(\rho_A)/n)+(1-\eta)g^{-1}(S(\rho_B)/n).
\]

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Diagonal form comparisons are interpolated through a regularized entropy minimum. Its Hessian is controlled in a metric adapted to thermal relaxation, forcing the thermal extremizer and hence the inequality; fixed-entropy output and degraded broadcast-capacity consequences follow.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Independent finite-energy bosonic inputs are claimed to satisfy the entropy photon-number inequality in any finite number of modes, allowing internal multimode entanglement. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 273, the documented statement is the independent-input finite-energy multimode photon-number entropy inequality. Thermal-attenuator and broadcast-capacity consequences are not selected. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{EntropyPhotonNumber.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

